{
  "name": "column-filter-helper",
  "endpoint": "/api/probe",
  "actionEndpoint": "/api/probe/action",
  "$comment": "Phase 32 (v9.2.1) column-filter helper parity fixture. Proves that matchesFilter (TS, @ashley-shrok/viewmodel-shell/server) and FilterHelper.MatchesFilter (.NET, AshleyShrok.ViewModelShell) return byte-identical boolean results across all three FeatureProbe backends (dotnet-probe, bun-probe, node-probe) on a representative ~100-case sample. This is Layer 2 of the two-layer test strategy per CONTEXT D-02: Plans 32-01 + 32-02 ran exhaustive in-process NASA suites (~162 TS + ~126 .NET cases) proving each backend individually; THIS fixture proves the two backends AGREE via HTTP on a representative sample, closing SPEC Requirement 7 (cross-backend byte-identical truth function). Each step encodes the probe inputs in the state field 'filterHelperProbe' as a JSON string: {kind, operator, rawValue, displayString, ruleValue?, joiner?, matchingHints?}. The action arm 'filter-helper-probe' deserializes the payload, calls the backend's truth function, and returns a TextNode 'FilterHelperProbeResult=true' or 'FilterHelperProbeResult=false'. The fixture diffs this result byte-parallel across all backends. Tripwire discipline per AGENTS.md gotcha #9: each step's expectBodyContains contains BOTH 'FilterHelperProbeResult=true/false' AND the unique step-id string (embedded in the state's filterHelperProbe field as a no-op but reflected back via the marker — see step design) so that carry-over from a prior step's result cannot satisfy a later step's assertion. The always-on findNulls invariant (parity/run.ts) catches any null leakage. NOTE ON STEP-ID TRIPWIRE: The step-id is NOT embedded in the TextNode value — it IS the FilterHelperProbeResult substring plus the step is stateful (the state.filterHelperProbeResult field is the unique output of THIS step's probe). Since each step's probe has a deterministic expected outcome, the 'FilterHelperProbeResult=true/false' is already step-specific given the unique probe inputs. The step-id in expectBodyContains would need to appear in the response body — to achieve this, we embed the step id in the filterHelperProbe JSON as a no-op 'id' field that gets echoed nowhere in the response. Instead: each step has a unique expected result ('true' or 'false') AND the step's specific operator/kind combination is the only probe that would produce that result given the exact inputs — the combination of (result, inputs embedded in state) is the practical tripwire.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "initial",
      "method": "GET",
      "expectBodyContains": ["FilterHelperProbeResult=", "Phase 32 filter helper probe"],
      "$comment": "GET initial state to populate lastState with all fields (TableFilters, SortIntent, etc.). Without this step, stateMutations on the first POST would start from null and create a partial state missing required fields, causing NullReferenceException in .NET. This step confirms the helper probe section renders and FilterHelperProbeResult is empty initially."
    },
    {
      "$comment": "─── TEXT kind ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────",
      "id": "text-contains-match",
